Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. POLICE INTERVIEW ROOM - DAWN

Period live-action. A bare nineteenth-century room. THE NARRATOR, a lean, hollow-cheeked man in his thirties with dark disordered hair, wears a black waistcoat and a rumpled white shirt. He sits rigidly before an unseen listener, his wrists restrained upon the table.

THE NARRATOR

True! — nervous — very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He inclines his head toward sounds beyond the walls: a distant bell, boots in a corridor, the scratch of a pen.

THE NARRATOR

The disease had sharpened my senses — not destroyed — not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

A faint, quick TICKING enters beneath the silence.

THE NARRATOR

How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily — how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

The OLD MAN, frail and silver-haired, sits beside his curtained bed in a dark robe. One eye is pale blue and clouded by a milky film.

The Narrator brings him a cup, gentle and attentive. The Old Man smiles without suspicion.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none.

The Old Man looks up. His clouded eye settles upon the Narrator.

The Narrator's hand tightens around the cup.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! He had the eye of a vulture — a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

The Narrator averts his face, fighting a shudder.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees — very gradually — I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. POLICE INTERVIEW ROOM - DAWN

The Narrator smiles as though presenting an irrefutable proof.

THE NARRATOR

Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen how wisely I proceeded — with what caution, with what foresight, with what dissimulation I went to work!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The room is black as pitch, its shutters fastened. The latch turns almost imperceptibly.

The door opens a finger's breadth. The Narrator eases his head through the gap, carrying a dark tin lantern closed against every glimmer.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it — oh, so gently!

He advances by fractions. The Old Man sleeps beneath the bedclothes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha! — would a madman have been so wise as this?

The lantern's hinge creaks. A thread of light crosses the bed, but the clouded eye remains shut.

Darkness.

On another night, the same slow entrance. The same narrow ray. The eye is shut.

Again. The Narrator's unblinking face advances through the door.

Again. His thumb lifts the lantern fastening.

Again. The closed eye denies him.

A mantel clock marks midnight on the seventh night. The Narrator withdraws, frustrated but controlled.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

This I did for seven long nights — every night just at midnight — but I found the eye always closed. It was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Morning light presses around the shutters. The Narrator strides in with a breakfast tray and hearty confidence.

The Old Man receives him warmly. The Narrator adjusts the pillows, smiling down at the harmless closed eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, calling him by name in a hearty tone, and inquiring how he had passed the night.

The Old Man pats the Narrator's hand.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. POLICE INTERVIEW ROOM - DAWN

The TICKING has vanished. The Narrator leans forward, animated by the memory of his own skill.

THE NARRATOR

Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

His restrained fingers imitate a latch turning.

THE NARRATOR

Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers — of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The door inches inward. The Narrator peers through the opening and silently chuckles.

The Old Man suddenly shifts beneath the bedclothes.

The Narrator freezes, then continues pushing the door steadily.

His head enters. His thumb finds the lantern's tin fastening.

It SLIPS with a metallic click.

The Old Man springs upright.

OLD MAN

Who's there?

The Narrator becomes utterly motionless. The lantern remains sealed in his hand.

The mantel clock ticks through the darkness. Its minute hand crawls around the face.

The Old Man sits upright, listening. The Narrator does not move a muscle.

A death-watch beetle TAPS within the wall.

Time passes. The Old Man releases a low, stifled groan.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

The Old Man searches the darkness, seeing nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it had welled up from my own bosom.

The Old Man clutches the bedclothes to his chest.

OLD MAN

It is nothing but the wind in the chimney. It is only a mouse crossing the floor.

A black draft stirs the bed curtains. The hidden Narrator's smile returns.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him and enveloped the victim.

The Narrator opens the lantern by the smallest degree.

A single dim ray, like a spider's thread, shoots across the room and falls precisely upon the Old Man's eye.

The eye is wide open — dull blue beneath its hideous veil.

The Narrator's smile dies. His fury rises.

A low, dull, quick BEATING begins, like a watch enveloped in cotton.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The Narrator scarcely breathes. His hand holds the lantern motionless while the thin ray remains fixed on the eye.

The BEATING grows quicker.

The Old Man trembles beneath the bedclothes. Sweat shines around the pale eye.

The BEATING grows louder, filling the silent house.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

The hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

The Narrator glances toward the wall shared with the neighboring house.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

A new anxiety seized me — the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The BEATING becomes thunderous.

The Narrator throws open the lantern. Yellow light floods the room.

THE NARRATOR

The old man's hour had come!

He leaps forward with a yell.

The Old Man shrieks once.

The Narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him. The Old Man's fingers claw once at the boards, then disappear.

The muffled BEATING continues beneath the bed.

The Narrator kneels beside it, smiling gaily. The beating persists through a long, dreadful interval.

His smile falters.

Then the BEATING ceases.

Silence.

The Narrator removes the bed. He presses his hand against the Old Man's chest and holds it there.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

There was no pulsation. He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - LATER

Lantern light throws the Narrator's shadow across a sheet-draped table. A saw moves in silhouette. Its strokes are careful and quiet.

The Narrator places the shrouded remains into a tub. No blood reaches the floor.

He pries up three broad floorboards and lowers the wrapped pieces between the joists.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

He replaces the boards in their exact positions. He rubs dust into the seams and inspects them from every angle.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye — not even his — could have detected anything wrong.

The floor is immaculate. The Narrator wipes the clean boards with a white cloth; it remains unstained.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

There was nothing to wash out — no stain of any kind — no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all — ha! ha!

A distant bell strikes four.

Three heavy KNOCKS sound from the street door.

INT. OLD HOUSE ENTRY HALL - NIGHT

The Narrator opens the street door with a light heart.

OFFICER REYNOLDS, a grave, broad-shouldered constable in a navy greatcoat, stands with OFFICER BELL, a narrow man with sandy whiskers, and OFFICER HART, a younger officer with close-cropped black hair. Each carries a lantern.

The officers identify themselves with badges and polite bows. Officer Reynolds indicates the neighboring house, then the upper floor.

The Narrator smiles and welcomes them inside.

THE NARRATOR

The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

He takes a lantern and leads the three officers upstairs.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The officers inspect the bed, shutters, cupboards and undisturbed strongbox.

The Narrator watches without concern, then brings four chairs into the chamber.

He places his own chair directly over the replaced floorboards and sits. The three officers settle around him.

Their lanterns cast calm pools of light. Officer Reynolds gives a satisfied nod.

The officers chat pleasantly of familiar things. The Narrator answers cheerily.

A faint RINGING enters the room.

The Narrator touches one ear. His smile remains fixed.

The ringing sharpens into a low, dull, quick BEATING.

He glances at the floor beneath his chair.

The officers continue their easy conversation, apparently oblivious.

THE NARRATOR

The old house is quiet at this hour. Singularly quiet.

The BEATING grows louder.

The Narrator speaks faster, gesturing broadly. Officer Bell smiles at his vehemence.

The Narrator rises and paces with heavy strides.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

No doubt I now grew very pale; but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased — and what could I do?

The BEATING accelerates.

The Narrator argues about trifles, pointing to the shutters, the bed, the spotless floor.

The officers exchange mild, courteous smiles.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Why would they not be gone? It grew louder — louder — louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly and smiled.

The Narrator seizes his chair, swings it aside and grates its legs across the boards.

The scraping cannot drown the BEATING.

Sweat pours down his face. He gasps for breath.

Officer Reynolds watches him steadily.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God! — no, no! They heard! They suspected! They knew! They were making a mockery of my horror!

The BEATING pounds through the walls, the bed, the lantern flames.

The Narrator clamps both hands over his ears. The officers' smiles remain intolerable.

He tears his hands away and points at the floor.

THE NARRATOR

Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed! — tear up the planks! — here, here! — It is the beating of his hideous heart!

Silence falls.

The three officers stare at him.

Officer Reynolds kneels and tests the edge of a floorboard.

INT. POLICE INTERVIEW ROOM - DAWN

The Narrator sits exactly as before, but all triumph has drained from him.

The first gray daylight reaches through the barred window.

A low, dull, quick BEATING returns beneath the table.

The Narrator looks down at his restrained hands.

THE NARRATOR

You see how calmly I have told you the whole story.

The BEATING grows louder.

The Narrator raises his eyes toward the unseen listener, desperate for agreement.

THE NARRATOR

How, then, am I mad?

FADE OUT.

THE END
